#question(title: [Prove these statements are false with
counterexamples], centered: true)[
  Let $C$ be a curve. Let $P$ be a point in
  $C$.
  #v(1fr)

  + The line tangent to $C$ at $P$  \
     intersects $C$ at only one point: $P$.
  + If a line intersects $C$ only at $P$,  \
    then that line must be the tangent line
    to $C$ at $P$.
  + The tangent line to $C$ at $P$
    intersects $C$ at $P$  \
    and “does not cross” $C$ at $P$.  \
    (This means that, near $P$, it stays on
    one side of $C$.)
  + If a line intersects $C$ at $P$  \
    and “does not cross” $C$ at $P$,  \
    then it is the tangent line to $C$ at
    $P$.

  #v(1fr)
]
